\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"shaman mistaken foreign language word misuse 1915\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d10\"},\"ref\":\"d10\",\"snippet\":\" ... From my own perspective as an anthropologist working on Copper Inuit ethnohistory, the most exciting part of this book is the period from 13 April to 8 November 1915, when Jenness parted company with fellow expedition members on the mainland to travel for the spring, summer, and fall ... \",\"title\":\"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/download/64915/48829/184429\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d1\"},\"ref\":\"d1\",\"snippet\":\" ... astral travel and spiritual light. \\\"Through Eckankar, Morrisseau develops a vocabulary for his shamanism,\\\" wrote Greg Hill, and it is true that Morrisseau soon began to cast himself as a shaman artist beyond the strict protocols of Anishinaabe culture.\\n\\nIn the summer of 1978, Morrisseau, long fascinated with ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.aci-iac.ca/art-books/norval-morrisseau/biography/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d11\"},\"ref\":\"d11\",\"snippet\":\" ... College, University of Oxford\\nemployer: National Museum of Canada\\noccupation: Anthropologist\\npredecessor: Dr. Edward Sapir\\nspouse: Frances Eilleen Jenness\\nchildren: John L. Jenness, Stuart E. Jenness, Robert A. Jenness\\n\\nDiamond Jenness, (February 10, 1886, Wellington, New Zealand – November 29, 1969, Chelsea, Quebec, Canada) was one of Canada's ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_Jenness\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d6\"},\"ref\":\"d6\",\"snippet\":\" ... A female shaman is sometimes called a , which is not an actual Tungus term but simply shaman plus the Russian suffix (for feminine nouns).\\n\\nDefinitions\\n\\nThere is no single agreed-upon definition for the word \\\"shamanism\\\" among anthropologists. Anthropologist Manvir Singh argues that the most justifiable definition includes ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamanism\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d12\"},\"ref\":\"d12\",\"snippet\":\" ... Boyce, Royal Navy, born in Cape Town, South Africa, in 1943\\n\\n• Sir William Lawrence Bragg,British Physicist and Nobel Prize-Winner (1915),born in Adelaide, Australia, 1890.\\n\\n• Air Vice – Marshal Sir Christopher Joseph Quintin Brand, Royal Air Force: born in Kimberley, South Africa, in 1893.\\n\\n• Gyles Brandreth, British ... \",\"title\":\"https://peek-01.livejournal.com/64479.html\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"d0713f73a7c0793ea9f17e0146ca391b0c1c79d994ee75fd7c4afc4bf1002a30\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"11d07e310780e4f5ff85c3e57f87411680d3becf95a1f87b9e4df8d6591ef5cc\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"anthropologist 1886 shaman April November 1915\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_8034c74270b3421e84460eb2"},{"content":"I found a strong lead: Diamond Jenness, born February 10, 1886, an anthropologist. The search result mentions a period from 13 April to 8 November 1915 when Jenness traveled. Let me read the Wikipedia page to confirm details and find the shaman story.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d11\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_9d5ecf37a0f14d04b33f71c5","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d11\",\"document_chars\":35979,\"end\":3000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e1\",\"sha256\":\"b9b427d60fb0a7ff184c479397f59728493a8e35446fd05542f0d6118aebf69c\",\"snapshot\":\"237346b59dc624f079566d064de401b4387fd9cb14ff14376d48b1c0ba57867b\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Diamond Jenness - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases\\ndate: 2005-02-07\\n---\\nname: Diamond Jenness\\nalt: Colourized studio portrait of Diamond Jenness, circa 1950\\nbirth_date: 10 February 1886\\nbirth_place: Wellington, New Zealand\\ndeath_date: 29 November 1969\\ndeath_place: Chelsea, Quebec, Canada\\nresting_place: Beechwood Cemetery, Ottawa, Ontario, Canada\\nknown_for: His comprehensive early studies of Canada's First Nation's people and the Copper Inuit.\\neducation: University of New Zealand (from the constituent college in Wellington, then called Victoria University College)Balliol College, University of Oxford\\nemployer: National Museum of Canada\\noccupation: Anthropologist\\npredecessor: Dr. Edward Sapir\\nspouse: Frances Eilleen Jenness\\nchildren: John L. Jenness, Stuart E. Jenness, Robert A. Jenness\\n\\nDiamond Jenness, (February 10, 1886, Wellington, New Zealand – November 29, 1969, Chelsea, Quebec, Canada) was one of Canada's greatest early scientistsGranatstein, J., 1998. \\\"Sir William Logan\\\", Maclean's magazine, vol. 111, no. 26, (July 1), pp. 38–40. and a pioneer of Canadian anthropology.\\n\\nEarly life (1886–1910)\\n\\nFamily and childhood\\n\\nDiamond Jenness was the second youngest son in a middle-class family of ten children. His father's profession was that of a watchmaker/jeweler, though he also installed several clocks in municipal building towers in New Zealand. The family was encouraged to read, learn music, and engage in sports. Richling, in his biography \\\"In Twilight and in Dawn,\\\" writes that the young Jenness \\\"was a proficient outdoorsman and an accomplished sharpshooter,\\\" skills that helped prepare him for his experience in the arctic years later.\\n\\nEducation\\n\\nAt an early age, Jenness showed proficiency for learning. He earned his first scholarship at the age of twelve by entering a composition competition for children under fourteen. In those days, in New Zealand, secondary education was only available to the wealthier families, so this scholarship enabled Jenness to complete high school and three years of college. He finished his final year of secondary education with six prizes: mathematics, science, Latin, French and English, and was named top student. He attended Lower Hutt School, then Wellington College. He and sister May were the only two siblings to proceed on to college.\\n\\nJenness graduated from the University of New Zealand (from the constituent college then called Victoria University College) (B.A. 1907; M.A. 1908), receiving first class honors for both degrees. Then, when 22 years old, he received a scholarship that allowed him to pursue further education at Balliol College, University of Oxford (Diploma in Anthropology, 1910; M.A. 1916).\\n\\nCareer (1911–1948)\\n\\nField work – Northern D'Entrecasteaux\\n\\nFrom 1911 to 1912, as an Oxford Scholar, he studied a little-known group of people on the D'Entrecasteaux Islands in eastern Papua New Guinea. Jenness comments:\\n\\n:::\\\"They peered at me from out-of-the-way corners, or through the do\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":3000,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_9d5ecf37a0f14d04b33f71c5"},{"content":"Diamond Jenness (born Feb 10, 1886) is confirmed. He had three children (John L., Stuart E., Robert A. Jenness). Now I need to find the shaman story and the 1940 report. Let me search for the shaman anecdote and the report details.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Diamond Jenness shaman mistaken word Inuktitut\", \"Diamond Jenness angakok shaman word misuse\", \"Diamond Jenness 1915 Copper Inuit shaman mistaken\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_b2bba3a2e9814ece9bde2f76","index":0,"type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Diamond Jenness encouraged report 1940 quarterly journal\", \"Diamond Jenness 1940 report cultural discoveries journal 1888\", \"Jenness anthropologist 1940 published report quarterly journal\"]}","name":"search"},"id":"call_6fab800862004499a160be2a","index":1,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d13\"},\"ref\":\"d13\",\"snippet\":\" ... As the expedition's archaeologist, Graham had one main quest, set for him by Diamond Jenness at the National Museum in Ottawa: to unearth conclusive evidence of an ancient Arctic culture quite distinct from the so-called Thule culture described by Danish archaeologist Therkel Mathiassen. ... At Avvajja, [near ... \",\"title\":\"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/view/63558\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d10\"},\"ref\":\"d10\",\"snippet\":\" ... THE DIARY OF DIAMOND JENNESS, 1913-1916. Edited by STUARTE. JENNESS. Hull: Canadian Museum of Civilization, 1991. 902 p., 208 illus. (maps, photos and draw-ings), appendices, index, bib. Hardbound. Cdn$65.00. This superbly edited volume brings into public view the complete arctic diary of Diamond Jenness ... \",\"title\":\"https://journalhosting.ucalgary.ca/index.php/arctic/article/download/64915/48829/184429\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_rang